[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: pc0,r1,r11,r12,pc12
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: host OS kernel memory management on all path nodes (endpoints and routers). The network stack and routing daemons are healthy; the hiccups are caused by a periodic system task dropping kernel caches.

Mechanism: every ~2 minutes the nodes execute a cache purge (writing to /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches). That operation forces the kernel to reclaim page cache/dentries/inodes, briefly spiking CPU and stalling packet processing and userland I/O. During those short windows, TCP data paths “freeze” then recover once the system resumes normal scheduling, which matches the user’s symptoms.

Localization: evidence is present on all devices involved in the PC0↔PC12 path:
- pc0, r1, r11, r12, and pc12 all log “mini_init (288): drop_caches: 1” at 197063s and again at 197183s (≈120s cadence) in dmesg.
- Interfaces on r1/r11/r12 show zero errors/drops; OSPF/EIGRP adjacencies are stable; ARP/neighbors are healthy. This rules out L2/L3 faults and points to host-level periodic stalls.

Root cause: a periodic maintenance hook in the container init (“mini_init”) is flushing kernel caches on every node approximately every two minutes.

Corrective fix: disable the periodic drop_caches action across the lab nodes. Remove/comment any “echo 1 > /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches” loop from the base init/startup script (mini_init) or its timer, and ensure no cron/systemd task performs cache drops. As an immediate mitigation, stop that routine on the node template used to spawn pc0/r1/r11/r12/pc12 and rebuild/restart the lab. After removal, the intermittent freezes should cease, while routing and links require no changes. 
=== END ===